<p>When it comes to selling your home, first impressions can make or break a deal. In a competitive property market, buyers make snap judgements within seconds of walking through your front door — and often even before they get there. Home staging is the art of presenting your property in its very best light, helping potential buyers visualise themselves living there and, crucially, motivating them to make a strong offer. Done well, staging can add thousands to your sale price and slash the time your property sits on the market.</p>

<p>The good news is that effective staging doesn't have to cost a fortune. Many of the most impactful changes are free or very low cost. It's about editing, cleaning, and repositioning what you already have. In this guide, Ark Housing walks you through the key principles of home staging so you can walk into sale negotiations with the strongest possible hand.</p>

<h2>1. Declutter Ruthlessly — Then Declutter Again</h2>

<p>The single most impactful thing you can do before marketing your home is declutter. Buyers need to see the space, the light, and the potential of each room — not your collection of ornaments, piles of post, or overflowing wardrobes. Every surface should be nearly clear, and every room should feel spacious rather than crammed.</p>

<p>Go through each room methodically. Remove anything you don't use regularly, pack away personal photos and keepsakes, and be ruthless about furniture. If a room has too many pieces, consider putting some in storage temporarily. Furniture that's too large for a room makes the space feel cramped; furniture that's too small makes it feel sparse and unloved. Aim for that comfortable middle ground where the layout makes sense and movement feels natural.</p>

